Evaluation Priorities (always keep these in mind)

This project is judged in specific award categories — every architectural and code decision should be made with these in mind, not just "does it work": Best UI Design · Best Code Quality · Best Widget Architecture · Best Project Structure · Best GitHub Repository · Best README Documentation · Best Presentation · Best Problem Solving · Best Overall Project. Concretely: keep widgets small and reusable (Widget Architecture), keep the folder structure exactly as defined below with no ad-hoc files (Project Structure), write clean incremental git commits with clear messages (GitHub Repository), keep the README fully in sync with whatever is added (README Documentation), and prefer documented, deliberate choices over quick hacks (Problem Solving) — this is a portfolio piece, not just a passing submission.

Project Overview

"HomeServe" is a Flutter mobile app for a Home Services Platform (cleaning, maintenance, repairs, moving, security systems, daily services, outdoor services). The app covers four connected screens — Home, Service Listing, Service Details, and Booking — built entirely with dummy data (no backend/API). This is an internship deliverable at Eduzah, so code quality, clean architecture, and visual polish matter as much as functionality.

Architecture

Feature-based Clean Architecture, kept lightweight since there is no backend or business logic beyond navigation and UI state:

lib/
├── main.dart
├── app/
│   ├── app.dart                 # MaterialApp, theme, routing setup
│   ├── theme/
│   │   ├── app_colors.dart
│   │   ├── app_typography.dart
│   │   ├── app_spacing.dart     # 8px grid constants
│   │   └── app_theme.dart       # ThemeData (light + dark)
│   └── routes/
│       ├── app_routes.dart      # route name constants
│       └── page_transitions.dart # custom PageRouteBuilder helpers
├── core/
│   ├── widgets/                 # shared reusable widgets (buttons, cards, rating stars, section headers)
│   └── utils/                   # extensions, responsive helpers
├── features/
│   ├── home/
│   │   ├── presentation/
│   │   │   ├── screens/home_screen.dart
│   │   │   └── widgets/          # hero_section, services_row, testimonial_card...
│   │   └── data/                 # dummy category + testimonial data
│   ├── service_listing/
│   │   ├── presentation/
│   │   │   ├── screens/service_listing_screen.dart
│   │   │   ├── cubit/service_listing_cubit.dart   # search/filter UI state
│   │   │   └── widgets/
│   │   └── data/                 # dummy service data per category
│   ├── service_details/
│   │   ├── presentation/
│   │   │   ├── screens/service_details_screen.dart
│   │   │   └── widgets/
│   │   └── data/
│   └── booking/
│       ├── presentation/
│       │   ├── screens/booking_screen.dart
│       │   ├── cubit/booking_cubit.dart        # selected date/time/notes state
│       │   └── widgets/                        # date_time_card, numbered_section,
│       │                                       # booking_receipt_card, date_bottom_sheet,
│       │                                       # time_bottom_sheet
│       └── data/                               # dummy time-slot availability
└── models/
    ├── category_model.dart
    ├── service_model.dart
    ├── review_model.dart
    └── booking_model.dart

Each feature only has presentation/ + data/ (no domain layer, no repositories) — there is no real data source to abstract, so an extra domain layer would be pure boilerplate for this project. Keep it this simple unless a real backend is added later.

Tech Stack
Flutter (stable channel, latest version available at project start)
State management: Cubit (flutter_bloc package) — one Cubit per feature that needs shared UI state (e.g. ServiceListingCubit for search/filter state, ThemeCubit for light/dark mode). Keep Cubits thin: they hold and emit UI state only, no business logic beyond what this dummy-data app needs.
google_fonts — Poppins (headings) / Inter (body)
cached_network_image — for all dummy network images (categories, services, avatars)
Navigation: named routes via Navigator + custom PageRouteBuilder helpers in page_transitions.dart (no need for go_router — flow is linear: Home → Listing → Details)
Design System (must match exactly — no improvising new colors/values)
Colors: primary navy 
#0F2A4A, accent orange 
#F5843A, background white/off-white 
#FAFAFA, secondary text gray 
#7A828A. Dark theme: background 
#121417, surface 
#1E2124, text 
#F2F2F2, secondary text 
#9AA0A6, same orange accent in both themes.
Spacing: strict 8px grid (8, 16, 24, 32, 40) — no arbitrary padding/margin values.
Corner radius: 20-24px large cards (hero, gallery), 16px medium cards (service, testimonial), 12px buttons, 8px chips/tags.
Shadows: soft, low-opacity, multi-layer — never harsh default BoxShadow with high opacity/no blur.
Typography scale: H1 28/32, H2 22/26, H3 18/24, Body 14/20, Caption 12/16.
Booking screen specifically: full-bleed header photo with dark gradient overlay, a floating white card (16-20px overlap into the header) for date/time, numbered circular badges (orange) for the Address/Notes/Summary sections, and a receipt-style "Booking Summary" card with a thin orange left-border accent (not a solid dark block).
Conventions
Naming: PascalCase for widgets/classes, camelCase for variables/functions, files in snake_case.dart.
One widget per file for anything reused more than once; inline private widgets (prefixed _) only for single-use screen sections.
All dummy data lives in data/ files as static lists of typed models — never hardcode strings/prices directly inside widget build methods.
Every screen must be wrapped to be responsive (use MediaQuery/LayoutBuilder and relative sizing) — test against small phones and tablets mentally before considering a screen done.
Do
Follow the folder structure and design system values above exactly.
Build every repeated UI element (service card, category card, testimonial card, rating stars, CTA button, section header with "See All") as a reusable widget in core/widgets/ or the owning feature's widgets/ folder.
Use the Hero widget with a shared tag (e.g. service-image-${service.id}) on the service image in both the Listing card and the Details header, so the transition between them animates smoothly.
Implement page transitions via a custom PageRouteBuilder (fade + slight slide-up, ~250ms, Curves.easeOutCubic) used consistently for all navigation — not the Flutter default slide transition.
Implement dark mode via a ThemeCubit emitting ThemeMode + two ColorSchemes (light/dark) as defined above, toggle placed in the Profile tab or app bar.
Use BlocBuilder/BlocSelector to rebuild only the widgets that depend on a given Cubit's state — never wrap an entire screen in a BlocBuilder if only one small widget needs it.
Use const constructors wherever possible.
Keep every text block on Service Details short and scannable (e.g. description capped at 2-3 lines with a "Read more" expansion) — do not let any single section push the screen height unreasonably.
Add a short doc comment at the top of each new file explaining its purpose.
Wire the "Book Now" button on Service Details to navigate to the Booking screen (this is now a real navigation action, unlike in Task 2).
Implement date selection via showModalBottomSheet containing a custom calendar grid (selected day = solid orange circle with soft glow, today = thin orange ring), sized to ~55-60% of screen height (not full-screen) with the dimmed Booking screen visible behind it. Confirm button label: "Confirm Date" (not "Done"). The sheet itself is ALWAYS light/white background with navy text — never dark/black, regardless of the app's active theme mode — and every day cell must be tappable (verify onTap/GestureDetector is wired per cell, this has regressed before).
Implement time selection via showModalBottomSheet with a CupertinoDatePicker (mode: .time) or an equivalent wheel picker — the user picks an exact time themselves, not from a fixed list of slots. Confirm button label: "Confirm Time". Show a small header above the picker with the already-selected date (e.g. "Monday, Aug 24") so the user doesn't need to reopen the calendar to recall it. Do not change the picker's existing wheel style — only add this header. This header's text color must be theme-aware: white when the app's active ThemeMode is dark, navy when light — never a hardcoded color that can disappear against either background.
Both bottom sheets must blur the content behind them (not just dim/darken it) — use a BackdropFilter with ImageFilter.blur over the barrier, in addition to a semi-transparent dark overlay, to match the approved reference look.
Keep a BookingCubit holding the selected date, time, address, and notes as UI state, consumed by the Booking screen and its bottom sheets via BlocBuilder.
On tapping "Confirm Booking", show a modern custom SnackBar (floating behavior, rounded shape, soft shadow) with a success icon, a short confirmation message (e.g. "Booking confirmed! We'll see you Monday, Aug 24 at 11:00 AM"), and the accent orange or a success-green background per the design system — never the default flat Material SnackBar.
Don't
Don't add new packages without checking they're justified by this file's tech stack.
Don't use setState for anything beyond a single private widget's own trivial local UI state (e.g. an expand/collapse toggle) — use a Cubit for anything shared across widgets or that represents meaningful screen state.
Don't put business/UI logic directly in widgets — emit it from a Cubit and consume it via BlocBuilder/BlocConsumer so widgets stay declarative and testable.
Don't hardcode colors, spacing, or font sizes inline — always reference app_colors.dart / app_spacing.dart / app_typography.dart.
Don't create a vertical list for the Services section on Home — it must stay horizontally scrollable per the approved design.
Don't introduce a dark navy (or any) solid-color card background on the Home hero section — it must stay light/white, matching the rest of the app's cards.
Don't wire the "Confirm Booking" button on the Booking screen to any real action (no persistence, no API) — it's UI-only per the task spec; a simple dummy success state/snackbar is fine if you want visual feedback, but nothing is actually saved.
Don't use a fixed list of pre-set date/time chips for the Booking screen — the user must be able to pick any date/time via the calendar and time-wheel bottom sheets.
Don't add backend calls, HTTP clients, or persistence — dummy data only.
Don't use print() for debugging — use debugPrint() or remove before committing.
Don't leave any screen non-responsive — no fixed pixel widths that break on smaller/larger devices.